                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "1baaf1a3-a58c-3be2-801c-f8dcd18ca12e",
                        "entry_id": "856550db-33c8-30ef-8d16-254c19f5b12c",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A dance move in which the dancer slides backwards though the feet move as if walking forwards; the backslide."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "bea78a94-936b-3e59-ab13-e81fb2cd8e2c",
                        "entry_id": "856550db-33c8-30ef-8d16-254c19f5b12c",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A dance style in which the dancer appears to be moving in a low gravity environment."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "aa307ca3-3ef9-43ad-9e03-016cc8745dd0",
                                "sense_id": "bea78a94-936b-3e59-ab13-e81fb2cd8e2c",
                                "text": "Though the idea of placing wacky made-up characters in a real-life context was carried over from Da Ali G Show—wherein Buzz Aldrin was once asked if he was upset that Michael Jackson got all the credit for inventing the moonwalk—Sacha Baron Cohen’s Borat felt like something new, an attempt to square an improvised, guerrilla style of underground comedy with reality-TV stunt shows like Jackass or Fear Factor."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
